==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱerd-}}
* First attested in the 1610s.
* From {{der|en|fr|accréditer}}, from {{m|fr|a|à|to}} + {{m|fr|créditer|to credit}}, from {{m|fr|credit|crédit|credit}}.
* See {{m|en|credit}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkɹɛd.ɪt/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-accredit.ogg|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[ascribe]]; [[attribute]]; credit with.
#:''Nationalisms often '''accredit''' all the unemployment issues to immigration.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put or bring into [[credit]]; to [[invest]] with credit or [[authority]]; to [[sanction]].
#* '''February 17, 1793''', [[w:William Cowper|William Cowper]], ''letter to Samuel Rose, Esq.''
#*: His censure will {{...}} '''accredit''' his praises.
#* {{RQ:Cervantes Shelton Don Quixote|passage=these reasons {{...}} which '''accredit''' and fortify mine opinion.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To send with [[letter]]s [[credential]], as an [[ambassador]], [[envoy]], or [[diplomatic]] agent; to [[authorize]], as a [[messenger]] or [[delegate]].
#* {{RQ:Froude England|passage=Beton {{...}} was '''accredited''' to the Court of France.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[believe]]; to put trust in.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1820|author=w:Robert Southey|title=The Life of Wesley; and Rise and Progress of Methodism
|passage=He '''accredited''' and repeated stories of apparitions and witchcraft.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1855|author=w:George Cornewall Lewis|title=Enquiry into the Credibility of the Early Roman History
|passage=The version of early Roman history which was '''accredited''' in the fifth century.}}
#* {{RQ:Meredith Richard Feverel|chapter=15|text=He spoke as if he saw the Truth, and, persisting in it so long, he was '''accredited''' by those who did not understand him, and silenced them that did.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To enter on the credit side of an [[account book]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To certify as meeting a predetermined [[standard]]; to certify an educational institution as upholding the specified standards necessary for the students to advance.
#: {{ux|en|The school was an '''accredited''' college.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To recognize as outstanding.
# {{lb|en|transitive|literally}} To [[credit]].

